{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghQXrY3VDE4MN7G547ig","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For tetrahedral Co(II) I get **Dq ≈ 407 cm⁻¹ (Δt ≈ 4 070 cm⁻¹)** and **B ≈ 748 cm⁻¹**. These were worked out by hand; I did not use any tools.\n\n**How the bands are assigned**\n\nA tetrahedral d⁷ ion behaves like an octahedral d³ ion, because the sign of the field is reversed. The ground state is therefore ⁴A₂, and the three spin-allowed bands are:\n\n- ν₁ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂ = 10Dq = 4 070 cm⁻¹\n- ν₂ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(F) = 7 030 cm⁻¹\n- ν₃ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(P) = 16 400 cm⁻¹\n\nBoth ⁴T₁ energies come from the same formula, taking the minus sign for ν₂ and the plus sign for ν₃:\n\nE(⁴T₁) = 7.5B + 15Dq ∓ ½√(225B² − 180B·Dq + 100Dq²)\n\n**Solving**\n\n1. From ν₁: 10Dq = 4 070, so **Dq = 407 cm⁻¹**.\n2. Adding the two ⁴T₁ energies cancels the square-root term: ν₂ + ν₃ = 15B + 30Dq.\n   - 23 430 = 15B + 12 210\n   - **B ≈ 748 cm⁻¹**\n\n**Check**\n\nPutting Dq = 407 and B = 748 back into the formula:\n\n- The square root is √(8.765 × 10⁷) ≈ 9 362 cm⁻¹.\n- ν₂ = 11 715 − 4 681 ≈ 7 034 cm⁻¹ (observed 7 030).\n- ν₃ = 11 715 + 4 681 ≈ 16 396 cm⁻¹ (observed 16 400).\n\nAll three bands are reproduced to within about 5 cm⁻¹.\n\n**What B means here**\n\nThe free Co²⁺ ion has B ≈ 1 115 cm⁻¹ (standard literature value). The ratio β = 748/1 115 ≈ 0.67, which points to fairly covalent metal–ligand bonding.\n\nThe Racah parameter C cannot be found from these data, because only spin-allowed quartet bands were measured and their energies do not depend on C.\n\n{\"Dq\": 407, \"B\": 748, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Tetrahedral d7 bands assigned as 4A2→4T2, 4T1(F), 4T1(P); C is not determined by quartet bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7974,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7